                <pb facs="0500" n="400" sameAs="frus:frus1880/pg400"/>

                <head>No. 265.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Evarts to Mr. White.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1880-03-12"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 12,
                        1880</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 76.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch No. 87, of the 2d ultimo, in relation to spurious diplomas issued by a
                    so-called “American University at Philadelphia,” and reporting the injury which
                    the practice of issuing such diplomas is doing to the reputation of our country
                    in Germany.</p>
                <p>In reply, I have to inform you that I have communicated copies of your dispatch
                    and of its inclosures to the Secretary of the Interior, with an expression of
                    the hope that it may be found practicable to devise measures, through the Bureau
                    of Education, or otherwise, for the effectual suppression of the practice in
                    question.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WM. M. EVARTS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
